      "threads": [
        {
          "source": "",
          "thread": "Institutional capture via stVaults",
          "why": "Lido Institutional and V3 stVaults signal a deliberate pivot toward regulated enterprise clients, which readers recognized as a potential governance realignment away from retail DeFi values."
        },
        {
          "source": "",
          "thread": "EigenLayer competitive erosion",
          "why": "The $2.5bn ETH withdrawal headline tied outflows directly to liquid restaking rivals, framing Lido's dominance as structurally threatened rather than temporarily dented."
        },
        {
          "source": "",
          "thread": "Regulatory and legal liability",
          "why": "The California partnership-liability ruling and the SEC's securities allegation both attacked the fiction that a DAO shields participants from legal accountability, which readers found existentially novel."
        },
        {
          "source": "",
          "thread": "Governance architecture experiments",
          "why": "Dual governance, the L1do blockchain proposal, LDO staking, and the LayerZero-to-Wormhole bridge pivot collectively showed readers that Lido's governance is actively unsettled and consequential."
        },
        {
          "source": "",
          "thread": "stETH as DeFi reserve collateral",
          "why": "Aave V3 pools, crvUSD collateral proposals, and ARK's federal-funds-rate analogy positioned stETH as systemic infrastructure, not just a yield token, which raised the stakes of any protocol risk."
        },
        {
          "source": "",
          "thread": "Smart-contract and operational failures",
          "why": "The $24M trapped Lido staked SOL story and the attack-vector analysis piece attracted readers who wanted to understand whether Lido's scale amplifies rather than absorbs code risk."
        }
      ]
    },
